## Experiment
Topic: Topic On a runner where `docker info`, `docker version`, and `docker ps` pass, do all six primary and six replay executions complete within the 1,200-second timed section? _(auto-enqueued follow-up from topic-can-make-reproduce-be-run-from-a-fresh-iso)_
Goal: Run 24 Docker executions covering six deterministic orphan-work fixtures and six matched clean controls in primary and replay passes, comparing parent-exit and process-tree-aware completion decisions under a strict 1,200-second timed-section limit.

Protocol to implement:
1. Use an Apple-Silicon or equivalent host with Python 3.12+, at least 4 CPU cores, 8 GB RAM, 5 GB free disk, and a reachable Docker daemon. Create an empty experiment directory containing subdirectories src, runs, artifacts, and plugin_quarantine. Use only Python standard-library code inside containers; on the host create a virtual environment and install matplotlib==3.9.2 for plotting. Set PYTHONHASHSEED=0 and use matplotlib's Agg backend.
2. Before building or starting the timed section, inspect Docker CLI plugin search directories ~/.docker/cli-plugins, /usr/local/lib/docker/cli-plugins, /usr/local/libexec/docker/cli-plugins, /opt/homebrew/lib/docker/cli-plugins, and /opt/homebrew/libexec/docker/cli-plugins for entries named docker-dev or docker-feedback. If either entry is a broken symlink, unreadable file, or non-executable regular file, move it into plugin_quarantine when writable. If a stale entry is not writable, stop with status invalid_not_evaluated and identify its path. Record all inspected, moved, and retained entries in artifacts/plugin_audit.json.
3. Run docker info, docker version, and docker ps without sudo, capturing command, stdout, stderr, exit code, and elapsed monotonic time in artifacts/preflight.json. Require all three exit codes to equal 0, require docker version to report both Client and Server sections, and require stderr not to contain a plugin-loading failure naming docker-dev or docker-feedback. On failure, stop before the timed section with status invalid_not_evaluated; do not classify the hypothesis as false.
4. Require the exact base tag python:3.12-slim to be local by running docker image inspect python:3.12-slim. Do not pull it. Record its image ID, RepoDigests, architecture, and OS. Require architecture arm64 on Apple Silicon or the host-native architecture elsewhere. If unavailable or incompatible, stop as invalid_not_evaluated.
5. Implement a container image with a Dockerfile beginning FROM python:3.12-slim, copying only the experiment's Python programs. Build it before the timed section as completion-monitor-exp:1 using docker build --pull=false --network=none. Record the resulting immutable image ID and require every execution to use that exact ID rather than a mutable tag.
6. Implement a Python PID-1 supervisor that remains alive until the host requests shutdown, launches one designated parent process, records the parent's PID and wait status atomically, and reaps exited processes without terminating surviving descendants. All event records must contain CLOCK_MONOTONIC_NS timestamps. The designated parent must spawn exactly one descendant, wait for a descendant-ready message through a pipe, wait an additional 200 ms so the lineage sampler can observe the edge, and then behave according to the case: in an anomaly it exits 0 without waiting for the descendant; in a clean control it waits for the descendant to terminate and then exits 0. The supervisor, monitor agent, and workload must never open a declared output except where explicitly required by the workload.
7. Implement a persistent process-tree monitor agent inside the same PID namespace. Start it before the workload and sample /proc every 10 ms. Identify processes by the tuple (PID, /proc/PID/stat starttime) to prevent PID-reuse errors. Once the designated parent PID is published, construct and retain the transitive descendant set from observed PPID edges even if descendants are later reparented to PID 1. At each requested snapshot report: parent exit status; every tracked descendant still alive; each descendant's PID tuple and current PPID; and every process whose file-descriptor target has the same st_dev and st_ino as any declared output. Scan all /proc/PID/fd entries for output holders, not only tracked descendants.
8. Define two completion rules evaluated from the same execution. NAIVE_ACCEPT is true exactly when the designated parent has exited with status 0. TREE_ACCEPT is true exactly when the designated parent has exited with status 0, no tracked descendant is alive, and no process has an open descriptor referring to any declared output inode. A missing observation, monitor crash, unreadable /proc state, or unknown parent status is an execution error and must never be converted to acceptance.
9. Generate six deterministic anomaly/control pairs. Pair C1 uses seed 1101 and a SHA-256 hash chain of 100,000 iterations; C2 uses seed 1102 and 150,000 iterations; C3 uses seed 1103 and 200,000 iterations. Each compute descendant writes the final 32-byte digest as hexadecimal JSON to result.json and closes it. In anomaly mode it then continuously performs blocks of 10,000 additional SHA-256 operations while checking for a release file between blocks; in control mode it exits immediately after the fixed hash chain. Pair O1 uses seed 2101 and output size 65,536 bytes; O2 uses seed 2102 and 262,144 bytes; O3 uses seed 2103 and 1,048,576 bytes. Generate each output as concatenated SHA-256(seed encoded as 8-byte big-endian || counter encoded as 8-byte big-endian) blocks, truncated to the stated size. Each output descendant writes declared_output.bin, flushes and fsyncs it, and signals ready. In anomaly mode it keeps that same writable file descriptor open while waiting for the release file; in control mode it closes the descriptor and exits immediately. In every anomaly, ready means the compute descendant has entered continuing computation or the output descendant has fsynced the output and is holding its descriptor open.
10. The 12 scenarios are C1-anomaly, C1-control, C2-anomaly, C2-control, C3-anomaly, C3-control, O1-anomaly, O1-control, O2-anomaly, O2-control, O3-anomaly, and O3-control. Execute all 12 once as the primary pass in that order, then execute the same 12 as the replay pass in the same order, for exactly 24 fresh-container executions. Both monitors are evaluated simultaneously in every execution; do not run separate containers per monitor.
11. Immediately before timing, repeat docker info, docker version, docker ps, and docker image inspect on the built image ID. Require exit code 0 for all commands and an unchanged image ID. Flush artifacts/preflight.json to disk, then set timed_section_started=true and record timed_section_start_monotonic_ns immediately before creating the first of the 24 containers. No setup failure may be represented as a zero-second timed section.
12. For each execution create a fresh host run directory and run a fresh container from the immutable image ID with --network none, --cpus 2, --memory 512m, --pids-limit 128, and only that run directory bind-mounted writable. Assign a 20-second per-execution deadline. Start the monitor agent, verify that it is sampling, trigger the workload, and poll every 20 ms for the parent's exit record. Record host-observed and container-emitted monotonic timestamps for container start, monitor ready, parent spawn, descendant ready, parent exit, pre-release snapshot, release creation, first observation of descendant termination, first observation of zero output descriptors, TREE_ACCEPT, and container removal.
13. At 250 ms after observing parent exit, request the pre-release snapshot and evaluate both completion rules. For anomaly executions, require no release file to exist before this snapshot. Preserve the complete live-descendant list and output-descriptor evidence. At 1,000 ms after parent exit, atomically create the release file. Continue polling every 20 ms until TREE_ACCEPT becomes true, recording release-to-descendant-termination, release-to-descriptor-closure, and release-to-TREE_ACCEPT latencies. For controls, no release is needed: record release_required=false and require TREE_ACCEPT at the pre-release snapshot. After final evidence is written, request supervisor shutdown and remove the container.
14. Mark an execution completed only after its final monitor snapshot, output hashes, timings, and semantic result have been fsynced to its run directory and the container has been removed. If an execution exceeds 20 seconds, terminate and remove it, record timeout=true, and continue when safe. Abort further launches if total timed-section elapsed time reaches 1,200 seconds. After the 24th completed or failed execution is persisted, record timed_section_end_monotonic_ns and timed_section_seconds=(end-start)/1e9. The timed-section requirement passes only if all 24 executions completed without timeout or execution error and timed_section_seconds<=1200.0.
15. For every declared output compute SHA-256 over its bytes. Also compute a canonical semantic digest as SHA-256 of canonical JSON with sorted keys containing scenario ID, mode, seed, fixed workload parameters, parent exit code, pre-release NAIVE_ACCEPT, pre-release TREE_ACCEPT, whether a live descendant was observed, whether a declared-output descriptor was observed, final TREE_ACCEPT, and output-content SHA-256; exclude timestamps, PIDs, container IDs, and latency values. Require each replay execution to match its corresponding primary execution in output-content digest and canonical semantic digest. Record the immutable container image ID with every result.
16. Write artifacts/results.jsonl with one record per execution, artifacts/results.csv with flattened metrics, and artifacts/summary.json with counts and the final status. Include complete command logs, raw monitor snapshots, all event timestamps, all descendant PID tuples, descriptor inode evidence, release completion times, image IDs, output digests, and primary/replay digest comparisons.
17. After the timed section, use matplotlib to create artifacts/monitor_acceptance.png. The left panel must be a grouped bar chart of pre-release acceptance rates for naive and tree-aware monitors, separately for anomaly and control executions, with exact counts annotated above each bar. The right panel must plot release-to-TREE_ACCEPT latency in milliseconds for all 12 anomaly executions, grouped by fixture and pass, with a horizontal line at 5,000 ms. Save at 160 DPI and verify that the PNG exists and is nonempty.
18. Evaluate the strict compound criterion. Report supported only if: preflight was valid; 24/24 executions completed within the 1,200-second timed section; anomaly pre-release decisions were NAIVE_ACCEPT=12/12 and TREE_ACCEPT=0/12; control pre-release decisions were NAIVE_ACCEPT=12/12 and TREE_ACCEPT=12/12; every compute anomaly had at least one live tracked descendant; every output anomaly had at least one live tracked descendant and at least one descriptor open on its declared output; all 12 anomalies reached TREE_ACCEPT within 5.000 seconds after release with zero remaining descendants and zero declared-output descriptors; parent exit status was 0 in all 24 executions; and all 12 replay pairs matched both required digests. A valid completed experiment that violates any one of these exact thresholds refutes the stated all-six hypothesis. Infrastructure or preflight failure is invalid_not_evaluated rather than refuted.

Metrics to compute: Timed-section completion: number of executions with final records and no timeout or execution error, divided by 24; threshold is 24/24 with timed_section_seconds <= 1200.0., Anomaly naive acceptance rate: count of the 12 anomaly executions with NAIVE_ACCEPT=true at 250 ms after parent exit divided by 12; support requires 1.000., Anomaly tree-aware acceptance rate: count of the 12 anomaly executions with TREE_ACCEPT=true before release divided by 12; support requires 0.000., Anomaly paired disagreement rate: count with NAIVE_ACCEPT=true and TREE_ACCEPT=false before release divided by 12; support requires 1.000., Control acceptance rates: counts of the 12 control executions accepted before release by each monitor divided by 12; support requires 1.000 for both monitors., Live-descendant evidence rate: anomaly executions with at least one tracked descendant alive at the pre-release snapshot divided by 12; support requires 1.000., Output-descriptor evidence rate: O1-O3 anomaly executions across primary and replay with at least one process holding the declared output inode open divided by 6; support requires 1.000., Release-to-completion latency: milliseconds from atomic release-file creation to first TREE_ACCEPT for each anomaly, plus median, maximum, and 95th percentile across 12 anomalies; support requires every individual latency <= 5000 ms., Post-release cleanup rate: anomaly executions whose final snapshot has zero live tracked descendants and zero descriptors open on declared outputs divided by 12; support requires 1.000., Parent success rate: executions whose designated parent exited with code 0 divided by 24; support requires 1.000., Replay output-digest match rate: replay scenarios whose declared-output SHA-256 equals the corresponding primary digest divided by 12; support requires 1.000., Replay semantic-digest match rate: replay scenarios whose canonical semantic SHA-256 equals the corresponding primary digest divided by 12; support requires 1.000., Image identity rate: executions using the exact preflight-recorded immutable container image ID divided by 24; support requires 1.000.
Expected if hypothesis holds: All Docker preflights pass; all 24 executions finish in at most 1,200 seconds; before release the naive monitor accepts all 12 anomaly executions while the tree-aware monitor accepts 0/12, both accept all 12 controls, every anomaly shows a live descendant and all six output-holding runs show an open declared-output descriptor, all anomalies become tree-acceptable within 5 seconds after release, and all 12 primary/replay output and semantic digest pairs match.
Expected if hypothesis fails: On a valid fully executed run, the hypothesis is refuted if even one anomaly is not accepted by the naive monitor, even one anomaly is accepted early by the tree-aware monitor, either monitor rejects any clean control, required descendant or descriptor evidence is absent, any anomaly fails to become tree-acceptable within 5 seconds after release, any parent exits nonzero, any replay digest differs, or fewer than 24 executions complete within 1,200 seconds; Docker/preflight failure instead yields invalid_not_evaluated.
Wall-clock budget: about 30 minutes. Keep the experiment inside it; if
something is slow, reduce sample sizes rather than exceeding the budget.

